    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d607">

                <pb facs="0731" n="725" xml:id="pg725"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.248/244a: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Standley</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-09-26T18:00:00-04:00">September 26,
                            1942—6 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>278. In confirmation of third paragraph, Section 3, your 371, September 25 [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">23</hi>], midnight.<anchor n="27" xml:id="d607-f1"/> For
                    your strictly confidential information Department on inquiry has just learned
                    from War Department that on September 19 General Belyaev, President Soviet
                    Government Purchasing Commission, informed authorities here that on orders from
                    his Government delivery of planes via Alaska had been stopped.<anchor n="28"
                        xml:id="d607-f2"/> No reasons were given for this decision.</p>
                <p>In regard to Molotov’s statement that the Soviets would fly out any planes we
                    made available in Alaska Department has learned from same source that a
                    consignment of fighter planes for Soviets is in Alaska ready for delivery and
                    only await Soviet arrangements for travel through Siberia. Department further
                    understands that it is not feasible to send these fighter planes through Siberia
                    unless they are escorted during the journey by bombers or transport planes which
                    could carry necessary extra equipment and fuel for the fighters. None of these
                    larger planes is now available in Alaska.</p>
                <p>Because of the stop order of Soviet Government all deliveries of Soviet earmarked
                    planes for Alaska already en route have been stopped.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d607-f1" n="27" xml:id="d607-f1-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg648">p. 648</ref>; see
                    paragraph beginning “I remarked”, <ref target="#pg649">p. 649</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d607-f2" n="28" xml:id="d607-f2-content">
                <p>In a later telegram, No. 869, October 10, 1942, from Kuibyshev, Ambassador
                    Standley stated that “General Bradley has informed, me that negotiations
                    regarding the use of the Siberian ferry service have been resumed and that 12
                    planes have already landed in Siberia:” (861.248/250)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
